 collection of temperature, humidity\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 and barometric pressure measuring devices. When using these instruments, here are some things to keep in mind:\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery731\footery748\margtsxn1120\margbsxn940\pard\plain\s11\ls23\ilvl0\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li1079\fi-360\ri929\sl249\slmult1\sb119\tx1079\f0\fs20\b0\i0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Unless you are very\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 close to sea level, an actual (observed or uncorrected) barometer will usually\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 read less than a TV or radio weather report barometer.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For elevations less than 5000 feet, an uncorrected barometer should read \f8\b1\i1 approximately \f0\b0\i0 0.1 \'93 Mercury\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 less for each 100 feet of elevation above sea level.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For example, if your barometer instrument is at 850 feet elevation and the closest weather station reports 30.46\'94 barometric pressure, your barometer should read \f8\b1\i1 approximately \f0\b0\i0 .85\'94 (850/100 x .1) less, or 30.46-.85= 29.61.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 It is useful to keep records of information like this (what your actual barometer reads versus what this simple calculation says it should approximately read) to see if the comparison is constantly\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 jumping around.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 If you always make the check at the same place (same elevation) like your home or shop, and the difference is varying high by .1\'94, than low by .2\'94, etc., you may want to have the barometer or altimeter checked out.\par\pard\plain\s11\ls23\ilvl0\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li1079\fi-360\ri1009\sl249\slmult1\sb1\tx1079\f0\fs20\b0\i0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 If\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 you find that you are making many\expnd-1\expndtw-6  \expnd0\expndtw0 adjustments to\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\exp